<p id="wordpress-image-help" class="small">Supports a simple <code>![Alternative text](https://example.com/photo.jpg)</code> alone in its own paragraph. The option affects WordPress copies, block downloads, and the WordPress kit only. It resets when the body changes, you leave WordPress, import or restore writing, or apply or clear a publishing profile. It is excluded from backups and profile files.</p>
<p class="small">No image is loaded here. These are links to files, not Media Library uploads. WordPress and browsers can request their URLs when you open the exported blocks. Use public HTTPS image files you have permission to publish. Inspect every image in WordPress; use Upload to Media Library if you want a copy stored on your site. Review alternative text, including intentionally decorative images.</p>
<p class="small">The reading preview, reading page, HTML source, formatted copies, and Word keep image syntax as text. Original Markdown keeps its references. Inline images among prose, images inside lists, headings, quotes or tables, reference images, titles, source HTML, and unsafe URLs are outside linked-image conversion. The references below are text only; availability, file type, content, and permissions are not verified.</p>
<p class="small">To add an image or replace an omitted Word image, use <a href="#image-editor-panel">Add or edit an article image</a> below the article. Edit buttons here open the same guided editor for supported paragraph positions.</p>
